# `model::Kind`

The six model families, in the order that fixes each family's parameter vector.

`Kind` names the family for [`Model::make`](Model/make.md) and reports it from [`Model::kind`](Model/kind.md). Each family has a fixed parameter order, used by `make`, by `parameters` (both reading and writing), and by every calibration that treats the model as a vector:

| Kind | Parameters, in order | Count |
|---|---|---|
| `BlackScholes` | `volatility` | 1 |
| `Sabr` | `alpha0, beta, nu, rho` | 4 |
| `Heston` | `kappa, theta, sigma, rho, v0` | 5 |
| `Bates` | `kappa, theta, sigma, rho, v0, jump_intensity, jump_mean, jump_vol` | 8 |
| `RoughHeston` | `kappa, theta, sigma, alpha, rho, v0` | 6 |
| `RoughBergomi` | `xi, eta, alpha, rho` | 4 |

Only these parameters are in the vector; the optional extras of each struct (`mu`, the variance floors, SABR's volatility type) are set on the struct itself.

## Fields

### `BlackScholes`

`enumerator`, default `0`.

Constant volatility; 1 parameter.

### `Sabr`

`enumerator`, default `1`.

Stochastic alpha-beta-rho; 4 parameters.

### `Heston`

`enumerator`, default `2`.

Mean-reverting stochastic variance; 5 parameters.

### `Bates`

`enumerator`, default `3`.

Heston with jumps; 8 parameters.

### `RoughHeston`

`enumerator`, default `4`.

Heston with a rough kernel; 6 parameters.

### `RoughBergomi`

`enumerator`, default `5`.

Rough Bergomi; 4 parameters.

## Guidance

Argument of `Model::make`; returned by `Model::kind`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Building each family by kind. The parameter count differs; the name follows.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <vector>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  struct Case { fin::model::Kind kind; std::vector<double> params; };
  const std::vector<Case> cases = {{fin::model::Kind::BlackScholes, {0.2}}, {fin::model::Kind::Sabr, {0.2, 1.0, 0.4, -0.3}}, {fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}},
                                   {fin::model::Kind::Bates, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04, 0.5, -0.1, 0.2}}, {fin::model::Kind::RoughHeston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.3, -0.7, 0.04}}, {fin::model::Kind::RoughBergomi, {0.04, 1.5, -0.1, -0.7}}};
  for (const Case &c : cases) {
    const mod::Model m = fin::model::make(c.kind, c.params);
    out.writeln(m.name(), ": kind", static_cast<int>(m.kind()), ", parameters", m.parameters().numel());
  }
}
```

### Example 2

The order is the contract. Reading a Heston model back gives the five numbers in `kappa, theta, sigma, rho, v0` order.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(3));
  const mod::Model m = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04});
  out.writeln(m.parameters().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

A wrong count is refused with the expected order in the message.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  try {
    (void)(fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3}));
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
}
```

### Example 4

The kind of a model is recoverable: `Model::kind()` returns it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const mod::Model m = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Sabr, {0.2, 1.0, 0.4, -0.3});
  out.writeln("is Sabr:", m.kind() == fin::model::Kind::Sabr, ", is Heston:", m.kind() == fin::model::Kind::Heston);
}
```

